  "content": "Approach play is where the team loses the most strokes, and putting is second.\n\nThe season read is thin. It covers 11 rounds by one player, from Aug 21 to Oct 21. In that read, approach is -4.37 strokes per round, putting is -2.4, and off the tee is -1.36. Around the green is roughly even at 0.07.\n\nThe broader read is more reliable for the team. It covers each player's newest ten rounds, 55 rounds by 7 players, from May 29 to Oct 21. Approach is -3.09 strokes per round and putting is -3.03. Around the green is -0.1, and off the tee is the only area above zero at 0.4. Strokes gained has no college figure, so these are measured against the Tour baseline of zero. Behind that baseline is expected for a college team, and these figures don't place you against other Division I teams.\n\nAudit Testplayer has no recorded rounds, so the last-ten read covers 7 of your 8 players. The last recorded rounds for most players are from Sep 26, and Cole Bennett's are from Oct 21. That is why the season window holds only him.\n\nApproach and putting are close in size. Approach is the larger leak, but the gap between them is small. A look at approach by distance band, or at putting by distance, would show where the strokes go within each area.",
